$\mathbf{(H_{2}N M e_{2})_{4}F e_{3}(C l_{2}d h b q)_{3}(S O_{4})_{2}(D M F)_{1.4}(T H F)_{1.0}}$ (2-SO4). Under Ar, $100~\mathrm{mL}$ of anhydrous, degassed DMF was added to a flame-dried Schlenk flask. To this flask was added chloranilic acid $(168~\mathrm{mg},0.800~\mathrm{mmol})$ and $\mathrm{FeSO_{4}{\cdot}7H_{2}O}$ ( $345~\mathrm{mg}$ , 1.24 mmol). The reaction was heated to $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with stirring for 24 h. The reaction was subsequently allowed to cool, and the black solid was allowed to settle to the bottom of the flask. The DMF was decanted and then replaced with $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of fresh DMF. This washing procedure was repeated two more times, and then the flask was transferred to an Ar-filled glove box. The microcrystalline powder was then filtered and rinsed with THF, and then soaked in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of THF at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ , during which time the solvent was replaced with fresh THF three times. The material was then activated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under reduced pressure to afford $\bf{2-S O_{4}}$ as a black powder. Yield: $124~{\mathrm{mg}}$ $(34\%)$ . Anal. calcd. for $\mathrm{C_{34.5}C l_{6}F e_{3}H_{50.5}N_{5.4}O_{22.5}S_{2}}$ : C, 30.8; H, 3.8; N, 5.6; S, 4.7. Found: C, 31.3; H, 3.4; N, 5.7; S, 4.3. IR (solid-ATR) 1508 (s), 1483 (s), 1351 (m), 1315 (s), 1182 (w), 1052 (s), 999 (s), 899 (w), 854 (m), 642 (w), 573 (s), 508 (m) cm−1.
Preparation of single crystals of solvated $\mathbf{(H_{2}N M e_{2})_{4}F e_{3}(C l_{2}d h b q)_{3}(S O_{4})_{2}}$ (2-SO4). A thick-walled borosilicate tube was charged with $\mathrm{FeSO_{4}{\cdot}7H_{2}O}$ ( $12\mathrm{mg}$ , 0.041 mmol), chloranilic acid $(5.6~\mathrm{mg},0.027~\mathrm{mmol})$ , and anhydrous DMF $(3~\mathrm{mL}$ ). The purple solution was degassed with three freeze-pump-thaw cycles, and the tube was sealed under vacuum. The reaction was heated to $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ , and was then allowed to cool to ambient temperature to yield black, hexagonal single crystals of solvated 2-SO4.
Computational Details. Periodic DFT calculations were performed without any symmetry constraint using the Vienna ab initio Simulation Package (VASP).36 During the geometry optimizations, both atomic positions and the lattice parameters were allowed to relax. We employed the $\mathrm{PBE}^{37}$ exchange−correlation functional plus U method with $520~\mathrm{eV}$ plane-wave cutoff (further discussion of the choice of U is included in the Supporting Information). A Hellman−Feynman force criterion of $0.05\mathrm{eV}/\mathrm{\AA}$ was used on each relaxed ion. For the electronic minimization, we used a convergence criterion of $10^{-6}\mathrm{eV}$ . The core−valence electron interactions were described by using the projector augmented wave (PAW) method38 with H (1s), C (2s, 2p), O (2s, 2p), Cl (3s, 3p), Fe (4s, 3d) and Cr (4s, 3d) electrons being treated as valence states. Dispersion effects were included through the D3-correction39 with Becke-Johnson damping.40 A $1\times1\times2\mathrm{k}$ -point mesh was used for the sampling of the first Brillouin zone41 during geometry optimization (see Table S3 for $\mathbf{k}$ -point mesh convergence).
